{"title":"2004 IMO Shortlist Problems/N3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solutions\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solutions</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Resources\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Resources</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>(<i>Mohsen Jamali, Iran</i>)\nA function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/2/8/228663427166fa0dd8911ea5e0d4c3ddfd5ded79.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"16\" /> from the set of positive integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/9/639a63f4b76f5cd6b54773491e1074fa937e2eaf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\mathbf{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"16\" height=\"13\" /> to itself is such that for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/0/3/c034476628c05a67e18fc4a1f4c36b768c7dbae8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m, n \\in \\mathbf{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"73\" height=\"16\" /> the number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9eef3f3b954a2deab29b574a1bb3dfe2f078d087.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle (m^2 +n)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"76\" height=\"19\" /> is divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/9/6091c1a50aa1ce79c31146e8923284c99726ad1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f^2(m) + f(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"105\" height=\"19\" />.  Prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/c/3acaad6617b287b21dfc6c4e84fda115fcd44e3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(n) = n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"70\" height=\"18\" /> for each <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/3/af32d90beeb20394f288a487d4b507b3484ef474.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n \\in \\mathbf{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"51\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p><p><b>Comment by the proposer.</b> A possible version of this question is:\n</p><p>Find all functions <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/a/f/1aff6d1f17d7fb8881435ca96ed4ac41898b81b0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f :  \\mathbf{N}\\rightarrow \\mathbf{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"86\" height=\"16\" /> such that for all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/b/a6ba6e10f75c645d94284601671b46fd6db04332.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m,n \\in \\mathbf{N}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" />, the number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/e/9eef3f3b954a2deab29b574a1bb3dfe2f078d087.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle (m^2 +n)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"76\" height=\"19\" /> is divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/9/6091c1a50aa1ce79c31146e8923284c99726ad1a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f^2(m) + f(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"105\" height=\"19\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n<i>This alternative form was also Problem 3 of the 2005 5th Romanian <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=TST\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"TST\">TST</a>, Problem 1 of the 3rd independent study of the 2005 3rd Taiwan TST, and Problem 3 of the 2005 French Mathematical Olympiad, Dossier 4.</i>\n</p><p><i>Note:  Although strictly speaking, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/4/074fc5c8fde64b20b521e684d0ff050eee7f0837.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f^2(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"41\" height=\"19\" /> denotes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/e/40e30c7d10976a802584e3c93f8e3c9ad6f47b2c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(f(x))$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"59\" height=\"18\" />, in this context it is clear that it means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/9/ad9a7c634a1e52058c3bfdff468d9ad001d8032f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle [f(x)]^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"55\" height=\"21\" />.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solutions\">Solutions</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solutions\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><b>Lemma 1.</b> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/4/3d430d244ebb4901b6970a15554b2f2d90907048.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(1) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i>  We must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/d/6cdf9a336494294ceb185656c5505e28747173d3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[f(1)]^2 + f(1) \\mid (1^2+1)^2 = 4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"226\" height=\"20\" />.  But for any integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/9/2/f920a5353024537e136fe36e38ef61615238955b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle y &gt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"42\" height=\"15\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/3/9f372b34bd1bd59fd6c25d2b64d3841e26dbba65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$y^2 + y \\ge 6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"81\" height=\"18\" />, so we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/d/4/3d430d244ebb4901b6970a15554b2f2d90907048.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(1) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" />.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p><b>Lemma 2.</b> For all natural <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/8/f28858529ba2f3ee37f206af5b80500685ed17e0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(n) \\le n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"19\" />, with equality only when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/b/e/1bec8f377aa28b9095a1efb9dc0b45670aaf4e96.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i> We note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/c/9ece9d7fe2f6fc5cdeb05a1d82d60dc26d53609c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle [f(n)]^2 + f(1) = [f(n)]^2 + 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"217\" height=\"20\" /> divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/d/4bdedf2faeecc0239eff46e53d1c1ab29bffe198.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle (n^2+1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"69\" height=\"19\" />.  But if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/7/0c7456226605e66968bed641890ed7c56b7c4ca3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(n) \\ge n^2+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"108\" height=\"19\" />, then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/a/2ea34ce211cb2fdb0fd29afc61120b99beb67af3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle [f(n)]^2 + 1 &gt; (n^2 + 1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"178\" height=\"20\" />, a contradiction.  Now, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/e/9ce4c907f2e5592f7daf5cb20eae157331d7ba2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n&gt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"43\" height=\"12\" />, then we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/2/1/521381a60446497b107bb40c8bf59bb8a8292ff6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(n) + 1 \\mid (n+1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"144\" height=\"19\" />; since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/1/7/717a7bc50ecc12a7280cb3b95349de22781aa0d0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle (n+1)^2/2 &lt; n^2 + 1 &lt; (n+1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"242\" height=\"19\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/4/de49cb8479fd70259f1ede830cb60cc4edb22e0e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n^2 +1 \\nmid (n+1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"127\" height=\"19\" />, so we know that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/8/0/280217c8beda3f0734f03a9e5f77628a09c66544.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(n) \\neq n^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"77\" height=\"19\" />.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p><b>Lemma 3.</b> For any prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/c/32cadd650d339f29c49609356ba3145894c5aa5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/a/26a5fa40c6b49ba3367faf1ca99905cd01b01207.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(p-1) = p-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i> We know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/c/a/bcacc9599d2406190e51df00f4fc19b4bf65e850.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$f(1) + f(p-1) = 1 + f(p-1) \\mid p^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"273\" height=\"19\" />.  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/2/9221343173c0b2409f350f12dbc16e0743f2508e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(p-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"64\" height=\"18\" /> is positive, either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/a/26a5fa40c6b49ba3367faf1ca99905cd01b01207.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(p-1) = p-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/f/54f017a992c919c206196c5d581d9f56adb2c6dc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(p-1) = p^2 -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"136\" height=\"19\" />.  But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/d/9/dd90fee04f6da715786c7c9285aa9f8ed5db6070.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle p^2 -1 &gt; (p-1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"133\" height=\"19\" />, so by Lemma 2, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/a/26a5fa40c6b49ba3367faf1ca99905cd01b01207.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(p-1) = p-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"129\" height=\"18\" />.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p>Now, for any natural number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> and any natural number <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/6/de61dde472a066a5b90a2ff0be7a2f756110011b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle k$\" width=\"9\" height=\"12\" /> that is one less than a prime, we have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/d/16d67f1ab367ed5604cf634180a4eabb68579428.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[f(k)]^2 + f(n) = k^2 + f(n) \\mid (k^2 + n)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"296\" height=\"20\" />.  But for some integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/4/734ef645c78704dbe5a136258a49d8425298f623.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle A$\" width=\"13\" height=\"13\" />,\n</p>\n<center> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/d/01da59a7f55e9aa25e9dd7aec58acaae15736f64.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle (k^2 + n)^2 = \\{[k^2 + f(n]+[n-f(n)]\\}^2 = A[k^2 + f(n)] + [n-f(n)]^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"533\" height=\"20\" />. </center>\n<p>Hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/e/1/5e1171fb95d7ea5e12df5b7be863b002e3e6857f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k^2 + f(n) \\mid [n-f(n)]^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"176\" height=\"20\" />.  This means that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/a/37af9b6e896c02fd0de47a6ad76d5bf86bdc3434.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle [n-f(n)]^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"85\" height=\"20\" /> has infinitely many divisors, i.e., it is equal to zero.  Hence for all natural <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/c/3acaad6617b287b21dfc6c4e84fda115fcd44e3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(n) = n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"70\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p>We use Lemmata 1&#8211;3 of the previous solution.\n</p><p>For natural <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, we define <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/0/7/4079d70400e5a46c9453f564031b7cb4f2587c13.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle \\varpi(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"39\" height=\"18\" /> to be product of all primes less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />.\n</p><p><b>Lemma 4.</b>  For all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/c/17c01c6c0999c4a4cee8ba3cca49c4366d318613.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge 17$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/f/a8f089ed82f1fad3e0b052fe47e112307a75eaed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle \\varpi(n) &gt; n^4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"85\" height=\"21\" />.\n</p>\n<p><i>Proof.</i>  We will use strong induction.  We note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/2/a928f1fe0efd9f20c1043856d65ece1865060de2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle 2 \\cdot 5 \\cdot 7 \\cdot 11 = 770 &gt; 2\\cdot 18^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"210\" height=\"18\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/1/631f5a9b89625ede0e1cb6386ca103a77bb0fba6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle 3 \\cdot 13 &gt; 18$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"82\" height=\"13\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee0b3804ef09f43776077aa6a305c545d215a47.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle 17 &gt; 18/2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"78\" height=\"18\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/8/2/882110639c601631a4dafbb57475ea685b4e186d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle \\varpi(18) = \\varpi(17) &gt; 18^4 &gt; 17^4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"218\" height=\"19\" />.  Now, for all integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/3/823c7e5f3e438df47d31787057197f898e497b65.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$19 \\le n \\le 36$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"95\" height=\"15\" />, </p><center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/f/4/4f4a779fb4d7d5a59460bdc5215075e3b46203ac.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\varpi(n) \\ge 19 \\cdot \\varpi(17) &gt; 2^4 \\cdot \\pi(17) &gt; 2^4\\cdot 18^4 = 36^4 \\ge n^4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"412\" height=\"19\" />.</center>\n<p>This establishes our base case.  Now, for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/a/aba1d57c1835f30b8c5b86f9dae2f9427803a4cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n &gt; 36$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"55\" height=\"15\" />, suppose that the lemma holds for all integers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/0/f/b0f246a76fe82acf7657ccec7e4237cfc3acf0fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$17 \\le k \\le n-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"118\" height=\"15\" />.  By <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Bertrand%27s_Postulate\" title=\"Bertrand&#39;s Postulate\">Bertrand's Postulate</a>, there exists a prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/d/0/5d0ed3ebb56a9cf9b3bdcf410d8f4c3f91ea5b8f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle p_1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"15\" height=\"11\" /> greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/4/b54be5acb55edf0427d07fda9f579a9169244738.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\lceil n/2 \\rceil$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"19\" /> and less than or equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />.  Thus\n</p>\n<center>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/7/5/f752798d7b761ea34e4609e3f12a17657beeecdc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\varpi(n) \\ge p_1 \\cdot \\varpi(\\lceil n/2 \\rceil) &gt; p_1 \\cdot (\\lceil n/2 \\rceil)^4 \\ge p_1 \\cdot (n/2)^4 &gt; n/2 \\cdot (n/2)^4 &gt; 16 \\cdot (n/2)^4 = n^4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"660\" height=\"20\" />.\n</p>\n</center>\n<p>Thus our lemma holds by strong induction.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p>We will now prove that for all natural <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/a/c/3acaad6617b287b21dfc6c4e84fda115fcd44e3e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(n) = n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"70\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p><p>If this is not true, then there is a least <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/9/739ff7242da42ed0dc30f3ab7c5e64c728342945.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> for which this is not true.\n</p><p><b>Lemma 5.</b> If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/9/739ff7242da42ed0dc30f3ab7c5e64c728342945.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> exists, then it is not prime.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i>  Suppose the contrary.  One of the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/c/5cc636fd9ababbf95b50dcd632f1e32054d6d711.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[f(n_0)]^2, [f(n_0)]^2 + 1, \\ldots, [f(n_0)]^2 + n_0 -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"333\" height=\"20\" /> must be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/9/739ff7242da42ed0dc30f3ab7c5e64c728342945.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" />.  But since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/7/2d76b89b24f519332306a925dd94d8cd37fc8aca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[f(n_0)]^2 +1 ,\\ldots, [f(n_0)]^2 + n_0-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"264\" height=\"20\" /> must divide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/6/6/a66087f2c87343c8f4ffb1a837b512dfa4f420f8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"${} (n_0^2+1)^2, \\ldots,  (n_0^2+n_0-1)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"225\" height=\"21\" />, none of which can be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/9/739ff7242da42ed0dc30f3ab7c5e64c728342945.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" />, we conclude that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/1/8/d18dc5cae07cdbaddf645f41665e6a55e5016555.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n_0 \\mid [f(n_0)]^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"93\" height=\"20\" /> and hence <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/a/9aab18caad81806cb906b4f03c9a94f4a0a19ae5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n_0 \\mid f(n_0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"75\" height=\"18\" />.  Furthermore, for any prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/0/320562bdc97fe4d2b85c76ede3a8a3c69176e516.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle p&lt; n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"51\" height=\"13\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/8/7/187cc36f9b539da00f5e912e0fec8fc43f1a1de3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle [f(n_0)]^2+p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"92\" height=\"20\" /> cannot be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/c/32cadd650d339f29c49609356ba3145894c5aa5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" />, since it is a divisor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/a/8/aa86929c8ad3ade4c11244a5e0c4a7d689f831ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle (n_0^2+p)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"73\" height=\"21\" />, which is not divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/c/32cadd650d339f29c49609356ba3145894c5aa5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" />, so <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/e/0/ee06ae35561b6e211fc8f64590b1f94acf36f665.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$p \\nmid f(n_0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" />.  Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/2/0a2498437e3f5d51664d70765c1166a3643bbbf3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(n_0) &lt; n_0^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"19\" />, it follows that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/9/739ff7242da42ed0dc30f3ab7c5e64c728342945.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> is the only prime divisor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/8/5f81963b01eec368cbdf6e8959557d671a7c6aee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(n_0)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"42\" height=\"18\" /> and again since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/a/2/0a2498437e3f5d51664d70765c1166a3643bbbf3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(n_0) &lt; n_0^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"19\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/2/442bb8d74603a10614360d7235a0b5620e30f691.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(n_0) = n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" />, a contradiction.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p><b>Lemma 6.</b>  If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/9/739ff7242da42ed0dc30f3ab7c5e64c728342945.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> exists, then none of the numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/a/bea2eb6b1e0b1e37eef92f5ffd69dd4d2ab096fe.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n_0^2+1, \\ldots, n_0^2 + n_0 -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"179\" height=\"19\" /> is prime.\n</p><p><i>Proof.</i> Suppose, on the contrary, that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/9/0b9317d972d69afde42ecf9d5a911657f212aabf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n_0^2 + k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"50\" height=\"19\" /> is prime, for some integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/8/e/58eea5d218657336b58710238db0286304503489.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1 \\le k \\le n_0-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"116\" height=\"15\" />.  We know <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/e/3fe5201f5730ac2bd1ecbea4e06d23980c01e414.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[f(n_0)]^2 + k \\mid p^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"123\" height=\"20\" />.  But since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/2/0/9208ab8bf8a8e8408167933febf380ae7bf561e9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle 1&lt; [f(n_0)]^2 +k &lt; n_0^4 + k &lt; (n_0^2+k)^2 = p^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"337\" height=\"20\" />, we must have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/a/e8a50140290a6eb2cf6f6b62f677c8741b38963d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle [f(n_0)]^2 +k = n_0^2 + k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"170\" height=\"21\" />, which implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/2/442bb8d74603a10614360d7235a0b5620e30f691.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(n_0) = n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"84\" height=\"18\" />, a contradiction.  <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Template:Halmos&amp;action=edit&amp;redlink=1\" class=\"new\" title=\"Template:Halmos (page does not exist)\">Template:Halmos</a>\n</p><p>We now claim that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/9/739ff7242da42ed0dc30f3ab7c5e64c728342945.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> does not exist.  Since neither <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/9/739ff7242da42ed0dc30f3ab7c5e64c728342945.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> nor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/b/5/bb5b1d537234d54c5c1b783884e0514c3e325e0c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n_0+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"48\" height=\"14\" /> may be prime (by Lemmata 5 and 3), the only possibilities for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/3/a/43a6f57eb5f5c5aff911fa12313b33bd18a56fe8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n_0 &lt; 17$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> are 8, 9, 14, and 15.  But <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/3/4/334f5f837bf40528f1c4055c1d18144039fc9aae.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle 8^2 + 3 = 67$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"90\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/3/b/13b62296b551fba7bd65d5f12e482a68af667215.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle 9^2 + 8 = 89$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"89\" height=\"16\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/1/5/f15738e96e2eff3b175d55bdb2f3fb37fec18053.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle 14^2+1 = 197$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"108\" height=\"16\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/e/8ae72a9dde9370f1f0bd808573577fc1019cbe9f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle 15^2 +2 = 227$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"108\" height=\"16\" /> are all prime, by Lemma 6, we conclude that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/c/17c01c6c0999c4a4cee8ba3cca49c4366d318613.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n\\ge 17$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"53\" height=\"14\" />.  But for each prime <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/c/32cadd650d339f29c49609356ba3145894c5aa5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" /> less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/9/739ff7242da42ed0dc30f3ab7c5e64c728342945.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" />, one of the numbers\n</p>\n<center><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/8/4/684b026415b8a25f8aaba34d9cb1cf3993015881.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[f(n_0)]^2+1, \\ldots, [f(n_0)]^2 + p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"225\" height=\"20\" /></center>\n<p>must be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/c/32cadd650d339f29c49609356ba3145894c5aa5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" />.  Since these divide <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/d/dcd7a08e4ee78dd22d665feb02888375c84cd494.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(n_0^2 + 1)^2, \\ldots, (n_0^2 + p)^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"181\" height=\"19\" />, the only one of these which can be divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/c/32cadd650d339f29c49609356ba3145894c5aa5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/8/d381490c7c79a0588deca751cfa41e3fadc94ecb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle [f(n_0)]^2 + k$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"92\" height=\"20\" />, where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/6/de61dde472a066a5b90a2ff0be7a2f756110011b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle k$\" width=\"9\" height=\"12\" /> is the integer between 1 and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/c/32cadd650d339f29c49609356ba3145894c5aa5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/a/c3aff7f5822579e45282f726172ba66ac91f3c6b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k \\equiv -n_0^2 \\pmod{p}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"137\" height=\"19\" />.  It follows that for all primes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/2/c/32cadd650d339f29c49609356ba3145894c5aa5d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle p$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"10\" height=\"11\" /> less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" />,\n</p>\n<center> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/5/ab5ce8d89d0fcd1076a91a4df13974276ff76270.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[f(n_0)]^2 \\equiv n_0^2 \\pmod{p}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"178\" height=\"21\" />. </center>\n<p>By the <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Chinese_Remainder_Theorem\" title=\"Chinese Remainder Theorem\">Chinese Remainder Theorem</a>, then,\n</p>\n<center> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/b/b/abb709d3fda09c05fbef106ae1e063a4d094926f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[f(n_0)]^2 \\equiv n_0^2 \\pmod{\\varpi(n_0)}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"217\" height=\"21\" />. </center>\n<p>But by Lemma 4, the only solutions to this congruence other than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/f/6cf3f55cada8ca0804676be3d2058e25b276403f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle [f(n_0)]^2 = n_0^2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"102\" height=\"20\" /> are negative numbers (which are clearly impossible), and solutions which imply <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/e/0/2e0576ac3ef3eba5870955ef022a74f89a004da8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle [f(n_0)^2] &gt; n^2 + n^4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"143\" height=\"20\" />.  But by Lemma 2, this implies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/5/8/958d33a7fd94ab7e9ffa197a46d7ea8fcbd509f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n^4 &gt; [f(n_0)]^2 &gt; n^2 + n^4$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"186\" height=\"20\" />, a contradiction.  Thus <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/3/9/739ff7242da42ed0dc30f3ab7c5e64c728342945.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n_0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"17\" height=\"10\" /> does not exist, so there is no integer <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/c/7/8c7447db63092942e0a39cfe01d3d94174a89707.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle n$\" width=\"10\" height=\"8\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/b/60b7c968e933eae9ff66c9c1957f28c7b716be67.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\displaystyle f(n) \\neq n$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"70\" height=\"18\" />.  Q.E.D.\n</p><p><br />\n<i>Alternate solutions are always welcome. If you have a different, elegant solution to this problem, please <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N3&amp;action=edit\">add it</a> to this page.</i>\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Resources\">Resources</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems/N3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Resources\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2004_IMO_Shortlist_Problems\" title=\"2004 IMO Shortlist Problems\">2004 IMO Shortlist Problems</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?p=169519#p169519\">Discussion on AoPS/MathLinks</a></li>\n<li><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/viewtopic.php?p=215196#p215196\">More Discussion</a></li></ul>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017113751\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.047 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.071 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 662/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 473/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 24047/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%   23.522      1 -total\n 23.58%    5.546      1 Template:Alternate_solutions\n 11.00%    2.587      6 Template:Halmos\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:5013:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017113751 and revision id 15222. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}